{"story_id":"KR-51002-052","title":"The Mysterious Hoi Tree","title_raw":"LII     THE MYSTERIOUS HOI TREE","notes":"","language":"en","original_language":"ko","country":"Korea","country_code":"KR","culture_region":"朝鮮半島","latitude":36.6,"longitude":127.9,"location_precision":"culture_region","map_location_type":"source_culture_region","collector":"Im Bang; Yi Ryuk","translator":"James S. Gale","publication_year":1913,"year_evidence":"扉に 1913(序文の署名は 1911)","book_id":"PG-51002","book_title":"Korean Folk Tales: Imps, Ghosts and Fairies","source_provider":"Project Gutenberg","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/51002","source_title":"Korean folk tales : $b Imps, ghosts and fairies","license_status":"PUBLIC_DOMAIN_US","license_name":"Public Domain (United States)","license_jurisdiction":"US","rights_evidence_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/51002","redistribution_allowed":true,"commercial_use":true,"derivative_use":true,"ml_processing_status":"reviewed","verification_date":"2026-09-08","word_count":236,"text":"Prince Pa-song's house was situated just inside of the great East Gate,\nand before it was a large Hoi tree. On a certain night the Prince's\nson-in-law was passing by the roadway that led in front of the archers'\npavilion. There he saw a great company of bowmen, more than he could\nnumber, all shooting together at the target. A moment later he saw\nthem practising riding, some throwing spears, some hurling bowls, some\nshooting from horseback, so that the road in front of the pavilion was\nblocked against all comers. Some shouted as he came by, \"Look at that\nimpudent rascal! He attempts to ride by without dismounting.\" They\ncaught him and beat him, paying no attention to his cries for mercy,\nand having no pity for the pain he suffered, till one tall fellow came\nout of their serried ranks and said in an angry voice to the crowd,\n\"He is my master; why do you treat him so?\" He undid his bonds,\ntook him by the arm and led him home. When the son-in-law reached\nthe gate he looked back and saw the man walk under the Hoi tree and\ndisappear. He then learned, too, that all the crowd of archers were\nspirits and not men, and that the tall one who had befriended him was a\nspirit too, and that he had come forth from their particular Hoi tree.\n\nYi Ryuk.\n\nLIII","cluster":1,"analysis":{"analysis_version":"1.0","embedding_model":"intfloat/multilingual-e5-small","themes":[],"motifs":[],"animals":[],"nature":[{"label":"木","count":3,"method":"count","confidence":1.0}],"events":[{"position":0.0,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.091,"label":"Journey","hits":1,"lift":74.33,"method":"count"},{"position":0.182,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.273,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.364,"label":"Journey","hits":1,"lift":74.33,"method":"count"},{"position":0.455,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.545,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.636,"label":"Conflict","hits":1,"lift":176.38,"method":"count"},{"position":0.727,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.818,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.909,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":1.0,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"}],"tension":[{"position":0.0,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.091,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.182,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.273,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.364,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.455,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.545,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.636,"tension":1.0,"raw":5.263,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.727,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.818,"tension":0.0,"raw":0.0,"m